Creating new style arrays inside the `.map()` callback creates new object references on every render, preventing reconciliation optimizations.

Memoize the style computation:

```tsx
const getLineStyle = useCallback((isLocked: boolean) => 
    isLocked ? [styles.interactiveStepHeaderStepLine, styles.interactiveStepHeaderLockedStepLine] : [styles.interactiveStepHeaderStepLine],
    [styles]
);

// Inside map:
{hasConnectingLine ? <View style={getLineStyle(isLockedLine)} /> : null}
```

Creating new style arrays inside the `.map()` callback creates new object references on every render, preventing reconciliation optimizations.

Memoize the style computation:

```tsx
const getTextStyle = useCallback((isLocked: boolean) => 
    isLocked ? [styles.interactiveStepHeaderStepText, styles.textSupporting] : [styles.interactiveStepHeaderStepText],
    [styles]
);

// Inside map:
style={getTextStyle(isLockedStep)}
```

Creating new style arrays with conditional styles inside the `.map()` callback creates new object references on every render for each list item, preventing reconciliation optimizations.

Memoize style computation or use a style getter function outside the map:

```tsx
const getButtonStyle = useCallback((isLocked: boolean, isCompleted: boolean, hasSelector: boolean) => {
    const styleArray = [styles.interactiveStepHeaderStepButton];
    if (isLocked) styleArray.push(styles.interactiveStepHeaderLockedStepButton);
    if (isCompleted) styleArray.push(styles.interactiveStepHeaderCompletedStepButton);
    if (!hasSelector) styleArray.push(styles.cursorDefault);
    return styleArray;
}, [styles]);

// Inside map:
style={getButtonStyle(isLockedStep, isCompletedStep, !!onStepSelected)}
```

Creating new style arrays inside the `.map()` callback creates new object references on every render, preventing React reconciliation optimizations for list items.

Compute styles outside the map callback or memoize them:

```tsx
const getStepContainerStyle = useCallback((hasLine: boolean) => 
    hasLine ? [styles.interactiveStepHeaderStepContainer, styles.flex1] : [styles.interactiveStepHeaderStepContainer],
    [styles]
);

// Inside map:
style={getStepContainerStyle(hasConnectingLine)}
```

Or use conditional rendering instead of conditional styles where possible.

Creating a new function inside the `.map()` callback causes React to treat each list item as changed on every render, preventing reconciliation optimizations.

Move `handleStepPress` outside the map callback by passing the index directly:

```tsx
const handleStepPress = useCallback((stepIndex: number) => {
    if (currentStepIndex < stepIndex || !onStepSelected) {
        return;
    }
    onStepSelected(stepIndex);
}, [currentStepIndex, onStepSelected]);

// Inside map:
onPress={() => handleStepPress(index)}
```

Creating a new array `[currentTransaction]` on each iteration of the map function (which generates list items) creates unnecessary object allocations and can impact performance when mapping over many items.

**Suggested fix:**
```tsx
// Pre-create the array outside the map, or pass the transaction directly if the function accepts it
const items: SplitListItemType[] = splitExpensesArray.map((item, index): SplitListItemType => {
    // ... existing code ...
    const currentTransactionArray = currentTransaction ? [currentTransaction] : [];

    return {
        // ... other properties ...
        isEditable: \!currentTransaction || isSplitAction(
            currentItemReport, 
            currentTransactionArray, 
            originalTransaction, 
            currentUserPersonalDetails.login ?? , 
            currentItemPolicy
        ),
    };
});
```

**Alternative fix (better):**
If possible, modify the `isSplitAction` function signature to accept a single transaction instead of an array, or check if it actually needs an array. Creating arrays in loops should be avoided when possible.

Creating a new object with spread operator inside renderItem forces React to treat each list item as changed on every render, preventing memoization and causing unnecessary re-renders.

**Suggested fix:** Calculate `shouldAnimateInHighlight` and `isSelected` outside renderItem or pass them as separate props instead of spreading into a new object.

```tsx
// Pass as separate props to ListItemRenderer
<ListItemRenderer
    ListItem={ListItem}
    item={item}
    shouldAnimateInHighlight={isItemHighlighted}
    isSelected={selected}
    // ... other props
/>
```

Creating a new object with the spread operator inside `renderItem` forces React to treat each item as changed on every render, preventing reconciliation optimizations and causing unnecessary re-renders of child components.

Instead, pass the original `item` and `label` as separate props:

```tsx
renderItem={({item}) => (
    <RenderItemRow
        item={item}
        label={`${item.label} spread`}
        accent={{
            tone: item.isActive ? 'emerald' : 'ash',
            emphasis: Math.random(),
        }}
    />
)}
```

Creating a new inline arrow function `() => toggleGroupSelection(group.transactions)` for each group creates a new function reference on every render, causing unnecessary re-renders of `MoneyRequestReportGroupHeader`.

**Suggested fix:** Memoize the callback or use a bound function:

```tsx
const toggleGroupSelection = useCallback(
    (groupTransactions: OnyxTypes.Transaction[]) => {
        const groupTransactionIDs = groupTransactions.filter((t) => !isTransactionPendingDelete(t)).map((t) => t.transactionID);
        const allSelected = groupTransactionIDs.every((id) => selectedTransactionIDs.includes(id));

        let newSelectedTransactionIDs = selectedTransactionIDs;
        if (allSelected) {
            newSelectedTransactionIDs = selectedTransactionIDs.filter((id) => !groupTransactionIDs.includes(id));
        } else {
            const idsToAdd = groupTransactionIDs.filter((id) => !selectedTransactionIDs.includes(id));
            newSelectedTransactionIDs = [...selectedTransactionIDs, ...idsToAdd];
        }
        setSelectedTransactions(newSelectedTransactionIDs);
    },
    [selectedTransactionIDs, setSelectedTransactions],
);

// In render, create a memoized callback per group:
const handleToggleGroup = useCallback(() => toggleGroupSelection(group.transactions), [group.transactions]);

<MoneyRequestReportGroupHeader
    onToggleSelection={handleToggleGroup}
    // ... other props
/>
```

Or better yet, pass the group key and look up transactions in the callback to avoid creating new arrays.

Creating a new object with the spread operator inside `renderItem` forces React to treat each list item as changed on every render, preventing reconciliation optimizations and causing unnecessary re-renders.

Move this object creation outside of `renderItem` or pass individual props directly:
```typescript
// Option 1: Pass individual props
<OptionRowLHNData
    style={styles.flex1}
    opacity={shouldShowRBRorGBRTooltip ? 1 : 0.9}
    ...
/>

// Option 2: Memoize outside renderItem
const getOptionStyles = useCallback((shouldShow: boolean) => ({
    ...styles.flex1, 
    opacity: shouldShow ? 1 : 0.9
}), [styles.flex1]);
```

The `map` function creates new objects with spread operators inside the `renderItem` equivalent context. Each list item creates a new object `{key: item.key, shouldShowRightIcon: true, ...}` on every render, forcing React to treat each item as changed and preventing reconciliation optimizations.

**Fix**: Extract the item props creation outside the render or memoize the item objects:

```tsx
const menuItems = useMemo(() => 
  listItems.map((item) => ({
    key: item.key,
    shouldShowRightIcon: true,
    viewMode: CONST.OPTION_MODE.COMPACT,
    title: item.name,
    description: item.value,
    onPress: () => setSelectedField(item.field)
  })), [listItems, setSelectedField]);

// Then in render:
{menuItems.map((item) => (
  <MenuItem {...item} />
))}
```
